─── app/services/scene_result_query.py:162-165 ───
[other · medium] started_from/started_to are passed as raw strings into `BagRecord.started_at` (a
DateTime column) with no parsing or format validation. On MySQL (strict mode) a malformed value
surfaces as SQL error 1292 → 500 instead of a 422; on SQLite it becomes a lexicographic TEXT
comparison that silently mis-filters for non-`YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` formats (e.g. ISO with `T` or a
`+08:00` offset). Also, a date-only `started_to` (e.g. `2026-09-22`) is interpreted as midnight,
excluding the rest of the day, whereas the Web-side `bag_search_service._parse_time` pads date-only
end values to 23:59:59.999999. Suggest parsing with `datetime.fromisoformat` here (raise 422 on
`ValueError`) and applying the same date-only end-padding so both entries agree on the time
contract.

+     def _parse_started(value: str, *, end: bool) -> datetime:
+         try:
+             parsed = datetime.fromisoformat(value)
+         except ValueError as exc:
+             raise HTTPException(422, f"采集时间格式应为 YYYY-MM-DD 或 YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS：{value}") from exc
+         if len(value) == 10 and end:
+             return parsed.replace(hour=23, minute=59, second=59, microsecond=999999)
+         return parsed
+ 
      if started_from:
-         q = q.where(BagRecord.started_at >= started_from)
+         q = q.where(BagRecord.started_at >= _parse_started(started_from, end=False))
      if started_to:
-         q = q.where(BagRecord.started_at <= started_to)
+         q = q.where(BagRecord.started_at <= _parse_started(started_to, end=True))


