[bug · low] The Python-side classification intentionally mirrors the SQL CASE, but branch 1
diverges: `data_kind_case()` classifies a bag as `scene_slice` by `bag_uid IN (SELECT child_bag_uid
FROM bag_derivation)`, while this loop classifies by `view == "slices" or bag_role ==
"SCENE_SLICE"`. For a SCENE_SLICE bag with no derivation row (a state this same file explicitly
handles as legitimate in `bag_lineage_view`/`lineage`, which return 200 with null lineage when `der
is None`), the row displays `data_kind: "scene_slice"` here, but a `data_kind=scene_slice` filter
(SQL CASE) would classify it as `self_built_original` and exclude it — violating the stated
invariant that the in-row field and the filter share one criterion. Suggest deriving branch 1 from
the same source: batch-fetch the page's derivation child uids (`SELECT child_bag_uid WHERE
child_bag_uid IN (...page uids...)`) and test `bag.bag_uid in row_slice_uids` instead of the role
check.


